# Production environment for the Docker prod stack — TEMPLATE ONLY.
#
# The real file lives OUTSIDE this repository, at:
#   ~/.config/chatofy/prod.env   (chmod 600)
#
# It is deliberately not in the repo and deliberately not in GitHub Actions
# secrets. The deploy target is the same machine that runs the workflow, so
# routing these values through GitHub would add exposure (workflow logs, GitHub
# itself) and buy nothing. This template exists so the KEY LIST stays reviewable
# in version control even though the values never are.
#
# Every compose invocation must pass it explicitly:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file ~/.config/chatofy/prod.env ...
#
# Note on this filename: .gitignore's `.env.*` rule matches `.env.prod` but NOT
# `prod.env` or `prod.env.example` — patterns only match basenames beginning
# `.env`. That cuts both ways. This template is committable with no negation
# needed, and a file named `prod.env` holding REAL credentials would sit in the
# tree fully stageable. Keep real values out of the repo entirely.

# Two line forms carry meaning, the same convention the other templates use:
#
#   KEY=              unset, and here that usually means "fill this in".
#   # KEY=value       optional, shown with the value it takes when unset. For
#                     the AI providers that is the default in
#                     apps/api/src/config/env.schema.ts; for the sidecar knobs
#                     it is the one docker-compose.prod.yml applies itself.
#
# The list is SHORTER than apps/api/.env.example on purpose. Everything derived
# from the compose topology — PORT, DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL, LOCAL_STT_URL,
# LOCAL_TTS_URL, TRUST_PROXY_HOPS, the postgres user and database — is pinned per
# service in docker-compose.prod.yml, where `environment:` outranks this file.
# Setting one of them here does nothing, so none of them appear. NODE_ENV is set
# to production by both images. Everything else with a default — in the schema,
# or in compose for the paths — is simply left out and takes that default.
#
# apps/api/src/config/env.schema.spec.ts checks this file: no live line may
# restate a schema default, and no key may appear that neither the schema nor
# the deployment reads.

# ── Origins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The web's canonical public origin, and the one place it is written.
#
# The API refuses to boot in production while this is still the default
# (http://localhost:3001). Every verification and password-reset link is built
# from it, and a plausible-looking wrong origin is worse than an obviously
# broken one.
#
# Three consumers, one value: the API builds mailed links from it, the web
# resolves `og:image` and its icons against it, and compose hands it to the web
# as AUTH_URL for Auth.js — see the Auth.js block below. Changing the domain is
# this line. The API also allows exactly this origin for CORS unless CORS_ORIGIN
# is set — which it is not, because the web is the only browser client.
WEB_BASE_URL=https://chatofy.quanganh208.dev

# Read at BUILD time by the web image, not at run time — Next inlines it and
# next.config.ts derives the CSP connect-src from it, including the wss://
# origin. Changing it is a rebuild, never a restart. Sibling hostname rather
# than api.chatofy.*: Cloudflare Universal SSL covers the apex plus one label
# only, so a two-label name has no certificate on the free plan.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=https://chatofy-api.quanganh208.dev

# ── Avatar storage (Cloudflare R2) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# ALL FIVE OR NONE. A partial set disables avatar upload and removal — the API
# still boots and logs one warning, and the two routes answer 409 with a message
# saying so. It never half-works: there is no state where bytes are stored and no
# URL can be composed.
#
# The token behind these should be scoped to this ONE bucket, Object Read &
# Write. The API never reads objects back — the browser fetches them from the
# public domain below — so nothing here justifies an account-wide key.
#
# ONE bucket, `chatofy`, shared by every feature AND by both environments, with
# keys namespaced per feature (`avatars/…`). Everything in it is world-readable
# by URL.
#
# R2 publishes a bucket as a unit and scopes tokens to a bucket, never to a
# prefix — so a prefix is a namespace and NOT an access boundary. Anything that
# must stay private needs a separate bucket.
#
# This block used to name conversation audio as exactly that case, "which the
# product promises never leaves the user's machine". BOTH halves of that changed.
# Conversation recordings now live in THIS bucket under `conversations/`, and the
# landing copy no longer makes that promise.
#
# So read the paragraph above as a live warning rather than as history: every
# conversation recording stored here is world-readable by URL, permanently, with
# no authentication and no revocation. The mitigation is 64 bits of entropy in the
# object key and an owner-scoped API route that is the only way the app reaches
# the bytes — see `conversation-audio.ts` and docs/system-architecture.md. The
# trade-off was taken deliberately; if it is ever revisited, a private bucket is a
# second `R2_*_BUCKET` here and one factory in `storage.module.ts`, NOT a data
# migration, because the column stores a key rather than a URL.
#
# Accepted trade-off: because development shares this bucket, the credentials in
# a developer's apps/api/.env can write and delete production avatars. Use a
# SEPARATE R2 token per environment (both scoped to this bucket) so a leaked one
# can be revoked without rotating the other. See docs/system-architecture.md.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=chatofy
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://chatofy-cdn.quanganh208.dev

# The bucket's PUBLIC custom domain — not the r2.cloudflarestorage.com endpoint,
# which is the S3 API and is not publicly readable.
#
# SIBLING hostname, for the same reason NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL is one: on the
# free plan Cloudflare Universal SSL covers the apex plus ONE label, so
# `cdn.chatofy.<apex>` has no certificate and every avatar fails TLS in the
# browser. `chatofy-cdn.<apex>` is one label and is covered.
#
# ONE variable, read by BOTH services: the api reads it at runtime through
# env_file and mints avatar URLs from it, and web takes it as a BUILD arg because
# next.config bakes it into the CSP's img-src. So changing the origin is a web
# REBUILD, not just an api restart — a restart alone looks like it worked while
# the deployed CSP still forbids every URL the api now mints. The deploy smoke
# asserts the served img-src against this value on every deploy.

# REDIS_URL is deliberately NOT set here. docker-compose.prod.yml pins it to
# `redis://redis:6379` under the api service's `environment:`, which outranks
# this file, so it is derived from the compose network and cannot drift — the
# same treatment DATABASE_URL gets and for the same reason.
#
# Set it here only when Redis moves off the compose network. The URL carries
# credentials if it ever needs them: redis://:password@host:port

# ── Secrets — generate fresh, never reuse the dev values ─────────────────────
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# The postgres user and database are both `chatofy`, fixed in compose; only the
# password is yours.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=
AUTH_SECRET=

# AUTH_URL is NOT set here. It is derived from WEB_BASE_URL above, by
# docker-compose.prod.yml's `environment:` block on the web service.
#
# The two are the same thing — the canonical public origin — and holding one
# value in two hand-edited keys only creates the chance of changing the domain
# in one of them. Auth.js reads the name `AUTH_URL` out of the environment and
# takes no configuration for it, so the name still has to reach the container;
# what it no longer has to do is appear in this file.
#
# It is REQUIRED, and `trustHost: true` does NOT remove the need for it. The web
# container sets HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0 and PORT=3001 so the standalone server binds
# every interface, and Auth.js derived its callback from those — sending Google
# `redirect_uri=https://0.0.0.0:3001/api/auth/callback/google`. Google rejects
# that as an OAuth policy violation (400 invalid_request), NOT as a
# redirect_uri mismatch, which is what makes it confusing: the Cloud Console
# shows the correct URIs and sign-in still fails.
#
# Runtime value, not build-time: changing WEB_BASE_URL needs a web restart,
# not a rebuild.

# ── Mail (Gmail SMTP) — required in production ──────────────────────────────
# The API refuses to boot in production without both. Host and port default to
# Gmail over SMTPS (smtp.gmail.com:465), and the From address is SMTP_USER —
# Gmail rewrites it to the authenticated account regardless.
#
# Blank and missing are the SAME thing, and both refuse to boot. That was not
# always true: keys present but EMPTY used to start the app cleanly and bind a
# real SMTP sender holding blank credentials, so every mail failed at Gmail and
# the first person to notice was a user whose link never arrived. Fixed in
# apps/api/src/modules/mail/mail.module.ts (`configured`), with regression tests.
#
# SMTP_PASS is a Gmail APP password (requires 2FA on that account; ordinary
# passwords are rejected).
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# ── Google sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The DEV client is reused here on purpose. A separate production client is not
# required by anything: one Google client carries several authorised redirect
# URIs, and the API's allowlist (GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS, comma-separated) can name
# more than one, so moving to a dedicated prod client later means adding a
# second id, not a migration.
#
# The one hard requirement is the redirect URI. Google matches it exactly and
# Auth.js does not override basePath, so this must be registered on the client
# or sign-in fails with redirect_uri_mismatch:
#
#   https://chatofy.quanganh208.dev/api/auth/callback/google
#
# Both web halves or neither — the button is offered only when both are set. The
# API accepts id_tokens for AUTH_GOOGLE_ID; set GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS only to accept
# more clients than that one.
#
# Set these BEFORE the web image is built if you want Google login: the config
# module evaluates at load time, so enabling it afterwards likely needs a
# rebuild rather than a restart.
#
# The trade-off accepted by reusing: a leaked dev client secret is also a leaked
# prod one. Judged acceptable for a private, single-developer project; revisit if
# that changes.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# ── AI providers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Speech runs locally in the sidecars; translation is the one cloud call left in
# a turn.
#
# Still required even though translation moved off Gemini: this key also
# authenticates the realtime and summarization providers, which are selected
# independently of the translation one. Reusing the dev key is fine, but note
# Gemini meters quota per PROJECT — a key from the same project shares one
# bucket with dev.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Translation runs on DeepSeek, reached through the OpenAI-compatible provider —
# the schema's default, so only the key appears below.
#
# Why it is not Gemini any more: measured 2026-09-22 on
# benchmarks/error-analysis/rows.jsonl, deepseek-flash answered at p50 720ms
# against 1114ms for gemini-3.5-flash-lite on the same corpus and the same
# prompt. Quality was scored on the same run and the sovereignty corpus found no
# refusal, no softening and no hedging across 84 translations.
#
# What it costs, and it is not a latency question: conversation content now
# reaches servers operated in China. That is a data-governance decision, not a
# quality one, and it is separate from Google's own free-tier terms. To fall
# back to Gemini, set AI_TRANSLATION_PROVIDER to gemini; nothing else has to
# change.
#
# ONE key for the whole OpenAI-compatible family, not one per host. The endpoint,
# the model and the host's flags are NOT here on purpose — a model id only means
# anything against the endpoint that serves it, so they live together beside the
# host's name in
# apps/api/src/modules/translate/providers/register-default-providers.ts.
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=

# Command Code, for AI_TRANSLATION_PROVIDER=commandcode only. That selection
# sends every translate-path call to Command Code first (deepseek-v4-flash-fast,
# then gpt-5.6-sol polishing each finished line) and to the deepseek row above,
# on OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY, whenever Command Code fails or its plan's usage
# window is spent. Deploy the web, extension and mobile builds that know
# `server.translation.polished` BEFORE switching: an older client reads that
# event as a broken connection. Meeting minutes move with it, to Command Code's
# google/gemini-3.7-flash, with GEMINI_API_KEY as their fallback; that key also
# stays Live mode's, which Command Code cannot serve.
# COMMAND_CODE_API_KEY=

# Meeting minutes follow AI_TRANSLATION_PROVIDER unless this names their host
# outright: commandcode (gemini-3.7-flash, GEMINI_API_KEY behind it) or gemini
# (Gemini direct only). Uncomment only to split minutes from translation.
# AI_SUMMARIZATION_PROVIDER=gemini

# The speech selections below are the schema's own defaults, so a copy of
# this file carries them for nothing. Uncomment one only to move that stage off
# its default.
# AI_STT_PROVIDER=local
# AI_TTS_PROVIDER=local

# Continuous speech-to-speech (`client.live.start`) is OFF in production: the
# image sets NODE_ENV=production and the API refuses live starts unless this is
# `enabled`. Meetings and the translate page do not use it. Uncomment only to
# roll that refusal back — no rebuild, an api restart picks it up.
# TRANSLATE_LIVE_MODE=enabled

# ── Paths ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file and the model directories default, in docker-compose.prod.yml, to
# $HOME/.config/chatofy/prod.env, $HOME/chatofy/models/stt and
# $HOME/chatofy/models/tts. Set CHATOFY_ENV_FILE, STT_MODELS_DIR or
# TTS_MODELS_DIR here only to move one — and then as an ABSOLUTE path:
# services/local-*/models is gitignored with zero tracked files, so a relative
# path resolves against the CD runner's own checkout, an empty directory, and
# the sidecars come up with no weights. Compose does not expand `~` either.

# Optional: CPU threads per speech engine in the PROD sidecars. Defaults to 4,
# half of dev's 8, because both stacks share the same 16 cores.
# PROD_LOCAL_STT_THREADS=4
# PROD_LOCAL_TTS_THREADS=4

# Optional: decodes the PROD STT sidecar runs at once per engine, through its
# one recognizer (engines/base.py records why this is a semaphore, not a pool
# of model copies). The old behavior — one decode at a time — is 1.
# PROD_LOCAL_STT_CONCURRENCY=4

# ── Video meetings (Cloudflare Realtime SFU) — required ─────────────────────
# Meeting media goes from each browser or phone straight to Cloudflare's SFU;
# this host only drives it over outbound HTTPS, so no port is opened here.
# Meetings always load: the API refuses to boot without the app id and secret
# from the dashboard's Realtime > Serverless SFU app.
CLOUDFLARE_REALTIME_APP_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_REALTIME_APP_SECRET=
# TURN relay for clients on networks that block UDP (Realtime > TURN Server).
# Free when used with the SFU. TURN is on only when both are set.
CLOUDFLARE_TURN_KEY_ID=
CLOUDFLARE_TURN_KEY_API_TOKEN=
